A||||7||sqoach||CORRECT procedure to do a compression test?||||5||
Z||000000||sqoach||03-20-2001||01:41 PM||||ok guys, i'm about to do a compressoin test so i need to make sure i do it right. i already know i need to take the spark plugs out (that's obvious) but do i need to disconnect anything else? and do i have to do anything else when i'm turning the key? just want to make sure i get accurate readings.<P>i've done it before and all i did was take out the spark plugs. then i would turn it over about 5-6 times until the needle stopped going up.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.JPG">  <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||5
Z||000001||862M4inCA||03-20-2001||01:48 PM||||that's about it. just remove all the plugs and crank the engine 5-6 times in each cylinder. Also, it might be a good idea to disconnect the coil while cranking. You wouldn't want a stray spark to ground out to the ignition module.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||03-20-2001||03:47 PM||||PULL OUT THE FUSE FOR THE FUEL PUMP!||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||03-20-2001||05:56 PM||||thanks guys.<P>well, surprisingly, it came out pretty good. but on the first cylinder, it read 90 then kept going up each test after that. i re-tested that cylinder 5 times until it 140 and didn't go any higher on the next test. could that be because the tester is brand new? bought it about 3 weeks ago.<P>on my last test for each cylinder, they were all between 130 and 140. so that's pretty much normal i guess.||||reg||1
Z||000004||dman||03-20-2001||06:11 PM||||Hi everyone. I'd like to suggest the following (for the next time you run a compression check). First get the car warmed up a little bit. Remove all the plugs. Don't forget to hold the throttle body open when taking your measurements.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||03-20-2001||06:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dman:<BR><B>Hi everyone. I'd like to suggest the following (for the next time you run a compression check). First get the car warmed up a little bit. Remove all the plugs. Don't forget to hold the throttle body open when taking your measurements.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>hey dman. may i ask what warming up the car and hold the throttle open will do? not that i think you're wrong. just wondering. thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||862M4inCA||03-20-2001||06:43 PM||||I think I see the lines that he's going along. Since your car will spend most of the time warmed up when running, and since parts have a tendancy to expand when they're warmed up it would provide a more acurate reading for each cylinder. Pro'lly not a HUGE difference, but if the car is running I can see the benefit of doing a "warm compression" test.<P><BR>Also, holding the throttle open allows more air to be drawn into the cylinders providing a lower resistance<p>[This message has been edited by 862M4inCA (edited 03-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-21-2001||01:43 AM||||Hold the throtle open when you crank the engine.  <P>My duke tests out between 150 and 160 psig depending on how fast I can get the plugs out.  Usually, it takes me a couple of beers and I get 150 psi.  Compression tests are something I like to do slowly . . . You have to prepare for the possibility of very bad news.<P>The manuals all say that the lowest and highest pressures between cylinders should not exceed 10% of the highest measurement.  In you case, the cylinders should all be within a total range of 13 psig (from 120 to 133 psig for example).  Also, the lowest cylinder should be above 100 psig.<P>So, it seem like you have acceptable compression to me.||||reg||1
